<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    What a demo describes, resolved into something drawable - and NOTHING about drawing it.

    Maps and their geometry, materials and their parameters, models and their skeletons, the scene
    at a tick, the camera. All of it pure computation over Core and Content.

    Split out of Viewer3D on 2026-08-22 (D59), and the check that made the split cheap is worth
    recording because it was better than anyone expected: of the 35 files moved here, FIVE mentioned
    a renderer or form type at all, and every one of those five was a COMMENT - "see
    WorldRenderer.DrawModel", the phrase "Program Files". Not one code dependency pointed upward.
    The layering was already right; it simply had no boundary to prove it.

    **The TargetFramework is the point of this project.** Viewer3D is net10.0-windows because it
    hosts WinForms, and that TFM cannot run on the Linux measurement boxes - so every file sharing
    that assembly was excluded from mutation testing and fuzzing for a reason that had nothing to do
    with the file. 506 of the viewer suite's 570 tests never touch Direct3D; they were locked to
    Windows by association alone.

    Nothing here may reference System.Windows.Forms or Silk.NET. That is enforced by this file
    rather than by discipline, which is the whole argument D54 records for choosing MVP.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Tf2DemoSalvage.Scene</RootNamespace>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <!--
      Three analyzer rules are suppressed here, and they are NOT the same kind of suppression.
      Splitting them out because "we turned the rule off" is exactly the note that rots.

      CA1027 - "mark enums with FlagsAttribute". A FALSE POSITIVE, demonstrably: TextureQuality's
      members are 0, 1024, 512 and 256, which are PIXEL DIMENSIONS. They are powers of two because
      texture sizes are, not because they combine, and OR-ing Medium with Low would be meaningless.

      CA1819 - "properties should not return arrays". Inapplicable rather than wrong: the two sites
      are per-vertex and per-lightmap data on the draw path, and D56 records the decision to keep
      that path low level - structs and arrays, no wrappers. Returning ReadOnlyCollection would
      allocate per access on exactly the code that decision was written to protect.

      CA1034 - "do not nest type". A JUDGEMENT CALL, and flagged as one rather than dressed up as a
      false positive. PropModels.SkinnedModel and PropModels.ModelFrames became externally visible
      when the Scene/Render seam was drawn (D59); the rule's own alternative - make them not
      externally visible - is unavailable, because the renderer genuinely consumes them. The name
      PropModels.ModelFrames also says which subsystem produced it, which is worth something. Both
      are large records with method bodies and ~62 call sites, so un-nesting is a real change with
      real brace-matching risk for a guideline aimed at published library APIs, which this seam is
      not. Revisit if Scene ever becomes a package anyone outside this solution consumes.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1027;CA1819;CA1034</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Tf2DemoSalvage.Scene.Tests" />

    <!-- The viewer's own suite still covers most of this while the split settles; it moves to
         Scene.Tests as the tests follow their subjects. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Tf2DemoSalvage.Viewer3D.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Core\Tf2DemoSalvage.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Content\Tf2DemoSalvage.Content.csproj" />

    <!-- Valve's bone pipeline (D88). Scene decides WHICH entities exist at a tick and what they are
         doing; Animation decides when their bones get built and what merges onto what. The
         direction matters: Animation must not reference Scene, because the engine's bone setup has
         no notion of a demo, a tick or a scene prop. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Animation\Tf2DemoSalvage.Animation.csproj" />

    <!-- The file logging provider and the retention helper it carries (D83). Referenced rather than
         reimplemented: this is where ILogger comes from once the conversion off the static
         ViewerLog reaches this project. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Logging\Tf2DemoSalvage.Logging.csproj" />

    <!-- For IGameSystem, the level lifecycle Valve's own systems implement. A true leaf with no
         ProjectReference of its own, so this can carry no cycle and drags nothing in behind it. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.GameSystems\Tf2DemoSalvage.GameSystems.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
